t of the focus here at  kopp is on countries and their  commitments those making a difference on  a smaller scale are also being  celebrated one step greener ambassadors  have been working with summit organizers  to inspire others to make changes to  help the planet  one of those is jasmine allen an  apprentice wind turbine technician  [Music]  i love my job because  i get to do something that is  contributing to a better future  [Music]  i think that everything i have the  opportunity to learn as exciting as the  technology is constantly changing so  everything is always new and you face  different challenges every day  [Music]  it's important to make a change so that  generations after us  still can have a brighter greener future  my advice would be to get involved take  the steps to make a difference  well joining me now to discuss the  climate issues of the day are acting  director at the energy and climate  intelligence unit sepi golzari monroe  and professor of energy policy at  university college london jim watson so  just the man for this question uh  obviously the focus was energy today but  the main announcements were focusing on  coal one of the four priorities that  boris johnson has set out coming into  this summit so how big a deal were those  announcements it is a big deal i mean  it's partly because we've got more  countries on board as well as actually  regions within countries so i watched  some of the plenary speeches on this and  it was really striking to get not only  national governments on the stage with  companies and then representatives at  areas such as silesia and poland which  is very dependent on coal mining and so  it's significant to get more of them in  with plans to get away from coal with  plans to stop financing coal in other  countries and some quite surprising  additions like vietnam like chile middle  income countries who've really struggled  to get past coal for quite a long time  and and you feel that that's starting to  tip 