step back and say well  actually it was only six short years ago  that the world was on a path to six  degrees of warming that's obviously very  dangerous then after the paris agreement  we were on a trajectory for four degrees  still still very troubling and in that  time in six short years just before we  started this conference we were on a 2.7  degree trajectory now five days into the  conference with the pledges that have  been made by countries and these deals  that have been done outside of the  formal negotiations we're potentially  looking at having closed the gap to 1.8  1.9 degrees now that's critical that  shows that actually these conferences  they do work they need the public  pressure from the outside but they do  work year on year they build on it and  they turn the political temperature up  to keep global warming down and that 1.8  figure is what's been put forward by the  iea the director of the iea fatty beryl  today the international energy agency i  mean very respected organization jim so  but then again presumably there are a  load of caveats this is if the pledges  are met if it's one thing pledging  something but some of these pledges  don't have detailed plans behind them i  mean are you confident that the pledges  that have been made can be delivered  i think partially confident you're  absolutely right i mean fatty bureau  kept his team up all night i think doing  that we've got some former colleagues of  mine work there and they said they were  working very hard to kind of just get a  sense of it and to come to that 1.8  degree result but they're are huge  caveats about deliverability and  measurement whether you're talking the  methane deal being able to deliver that  you know that requires you for example  to monitor everything from agriculture  emissions from brazil to long-distance  pipelines for gas from russia into  europe and those are notoriously leaky  and difficult to manage so there's lots  of questions underneath but i do share  the optimism that actually you know we  are starting to see these big  cross-cutting deals in big chunks of the  areas of emissions and if they're  delivered we are getting much closer to  where we need to be and what i would add  to that is actually what these  conferences need to continue to do it  doesn't mean that you know job done you  know we're on a good trajectory let's go  home actually year on year the task now  is to make sure countries come back to  the table that they are actually  delivering on their promises so that  needs to be monitored year on year this  is a purpose for the climate conferences  going forward and the public pressure  for that is there and will continue to  be there but actually also making sure  that the private sector deals and the  broader multilateral deals that have  been made alongside the formal  negotiations are also being delivered so  that is a job for year on year to come  back and make sure that we're delivering  on these promises do you think that'll  be agreed because we've got greta  thunderberg once again a little bit  skeptic skeptical about all this she's  she's tweeted talking about greenwashing  when when it comes to these  announcements i mean people have her  promises before haven't they i mean and  you need to take people with you don't  you yes you do you do and i my sort of  natural innate optimism about all this  is tempered a little i mean it's a long  time since i've been to a copy over a  decade but this does feel different  partly because the solutions are there  and demonstrated you know the costs of  renewable energy have tumbled you're  starting to see cheaper electricity  storage you're seeing countries move in  ways that they didn't 10 years ago but  she is absolutely right you need to hold  feet to the fire and that's through the  public pressure you mentioned and if  that isn't there and you don't come back  year after year to make sure it's done  then you know we will go home and then  wonder what's happened the next time we  all get together we must leave it there  jim watson steppi goldzari monroe thanks  both very much indeed  don't forget you can listen to a new  climate cast a podcast every day from  cop 26 wherever you normally get your  podcast from and you may well already be  watching this on our pop-up channel but  if you're not you can find uninterrupted  coverage from cop26 between 9am and 7pm  on the channel 525 all week thanks for  watching see you tomorrow  [Music]
 

